+The AVM1 header is 12 bytes:
+
+| Offset | Bytes | Meaning |
+|---:|---:|---|
+| 0 | 4 | ASCII `AVM1` |
+| 4 | 3 | Little-endian compressed payload length (uint24, nonzero) |
+| 7 | 1 | Flags: bit 0 loops; all other bits zero |
+| 8 | 4 | Little-endian output sample count (uint32, nonzero) |
+| 12 | length | Unchanged mixed-256 frame payload |
+
+The sample rate, frame size, and scale table are fixed by this format. Compared
+with the evaluation container it saves 22 bytes and adds an explicit payload
+length and loop flag. FX addresses and lengths remain 24 bits; output counts
+are 32 bits to support recordings longer than 65,535 samples.
+
+## Frame decoder
+
+Each frame begins with a signed seed byte and a parameter byte:
+
+| Parameter bits | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| 7..6 | 0=hold, 1=one-bit residual, 2=two-bit residual; 3 invalid |
+| 5..4 | 0=previous sample, 1=linear extrapolation, 2=half slope; 3 invalid |
+| 3..0 | Index into scales `1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12,15,18,22,26,31,36,42` |
+
+A hold frame requires parameter 0. Its seed repeats for the whole frame.
+Other frames emit the seed once, initialize both histories to it, and decode
+255 residuals (fewer in the final partial frame). One-bit codes select -S,+S;
+two-bit codes select -3S,-S,+S,+3S. Codes are packed least-significant bit
+first. Frame padding is zero and each frame starts at a byte boundary.
+
+Prediction uses signed 16-bit intermediates. Linear extrapolation is
+`previous + (previous - older)`; half slope is
+`previous + floor((previous - older) / 2)`. Add the residual before saturating
+once to [-128,127], then update reconstructed histories. The runtime never
+uses source PCM history. A partial final frame stops at the exact uint32
+sample count; looping discards its unused padding and starts the next seed.
+
+## Streaming and peripherals
+
+Timer1 COMPA emits the held sample at a fixed 14-cycle body latency, then
+decodes its successor. OCR1A=127 and prescaler=8 give a 1024 CPU-cycle period
+at 16 MHz. Timer4 retains differential PC6/PC7 PWM with its 250 kHz carrier.
+Timer0 independently maintains the 1 kHz millisecond clock. Timer1 phase
+alignment to Timer0 is preserved.
+
+Only the foreground reads FX, at interpreter stream restarts and idle wakeups.
+The ISR consumes a **64-byte compressed ring**, requests refill at <=32 bytes,
+and never uses SPI or a decoded PCM buffer. Independent uint8 producer/consumer
+heads publish complete chunks atomically; foreground reads are interruptible,
+at most 32 bytes, split at ring wrap and payload end. Looping may prefetch the
+beginning before the final samples finish, so no foreground rewind is required
+at the output boundary. Reads never extend beyond the declared payload.
+
+Persistent state occupies **99 bytes**, including the ring (35 bytes of control
+and decoder state). The scale table occupies 16 firmware bytes. A temporary
+12-byte header aliases the existing foreground service workspace. The ISR
+preserves all interrupted registers, SREG, and the guest flags, including when
+native libm temporarily borrows the usual zero register.
+
+On compressed-buffer underrun the player emits silence and freezes decoding
+until data arrives; its saturating diagnostic counter records the event. Bad
+native headers, unsupported frame parameters, and nonzero frame padding fail
+closed. As with other program-memory APIs, the caller must supply a valid
+asset pointer and a truthful header; the offline packer validates file bounds.
+
